/*
# Divider — the rule between things

A single line in the theme's `line` token, sized to the room it is given: drop one into a
stack and it spans the content, drop one into a row with `axis = y` and it stands on end.
It is a painted view, not a border — which is what lets it be a child in a list like any
other, and lets a layout space it like any other.

```declare
Card [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 280,
    Text [ text = "General" ],
    Divider [ ],
    Text [ text = "Advanced" ]
    ]
```

**`thickness` is logical pixels, not device pixels.** `1` is one of the units everything
else in a program is measured in, so on a 2× display the rule is two device pixels and
looks like every other 1px edge in the library — there is no hairline primitive here.

## theme
The provided design-token record the rule takes its `line` colour from — set once by the
app, read here by name. See `Control.theme`.

## width
Derived, not authored: the span across the rule on a horizontal divider, `thickness` on a
vertical one. **Leave it alone** — writing a width here fights the span, and a shorter
rule is what `inset` is for.

## height
The mirror of `width`: `thickness` on a horizontal rule, the span on a vertical one. Also
derived, and also not for setting — a divider is as long as the room it was given.

## axis
Which way the rule runs: `x` (the default) is horizontal and spans the parent's width; `y`
is vertical and spans its height. A bare literal, not a string. The span is the parent's
**content** extent — the room inside the parent's own `padding` — so a divider in a padded
card stops where the card's content does, rather than running out under the inset.

## thickness
The rule's weight in logical pixels. `1` is the library's hairline everywhere.

## inset
How far in from the **leading** edge the rule starts — the list-separator convention,
where a row's separator begins under the text rather than under the icon beside it. The
rule's far end stays put, so the inset shortens it. `0` (the default) is a full-span rule.

## color
The rule's colour, the theme's `line` by default. Set it for a rule that must read heavier
or lighter than the theme's edges — a section break against a row separator.
*/

include [ "control.declare" ]

class Divider extends View [
    theme: Theme = { provided("theme", SanFrancisco) },

    axis: Axis = x,
    thickness: number = 1,
    inset: number = 0,
    color: Color = { theme.line },

    // The room the rule spans: the parent's CONTENT BOX along the axis — its
    // extent less its own padding — so a divider inside a padded card is
    // exactly as wide as the card's content, with no arithmetic at the use
    // site, and with no interest in what (if anything) arranges the card.
    // Both reads are reactive, so it re-spans when either changes.

    span() {
        const p = this.parent as any
        if (p == null) return 0
        return p.contentBox(this.axis == "x" ? "width" : "height")
        },

    width = { axis == "x" ? span() : thickness },
    height = { axis == "x" ? thickness : span() },

    // the rule itself, so `inset` can move its leading edge without the Divider
    // authoring an `x` a parent layout may be arranging

    rule: View [
        x = { classroot.axis == "x" ? classroot.inset : 0 },
        y = { classroot.axis == "x" ? 0 : classroot.inset },
        width = { classroot.axis == "x" ? Math.max(0, parent.width - classroot.inset) : classroot.thickness },
        height = { classroot.axis == "x" ? classroot.thickness : Math.max(0, parent.height - classroot.inset) },
        fill = { classroot.color } ]
    ]
